<date>2026-11-09</date>
<releaseinfo>0.1.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&kappname; is an application for the Trinity Desktop Environment for talking
to artificial intelligence services: the free &horde;, a &koboldcpp; server
on your own computer or network, and any cloud service or server with an
OpenAI-compatible interface. You can chat, attach files, have images made
and have text read aloud.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>Trinity</keyword>
<keyword>AI</keyword>
<keyword>chat</keyword>
<keyword>LLM</keyword>
<keyword>AI Horde</keyword>
<keyword>KoboldCpp</keyword>
<keyword>OpenAI</keyword>
<keyword>tde-ai-client</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
&kappname; is a native &tde; program for working with AI language and image
models. You type a message, the service you have selected answers, and the
conversation is kept so that you can come back to it later.
</para>

<para>Three kinds of services are supported:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>&horde;</term>
<listitem><para>
A free service at <ulink url="https://aihorde.net">aihorde.net</ulink> where
volunteers lend their computers. It needs no account and nothing to be
installed, which makes it the service &kappname; uses when it is first
started. See <xref linkend="service-horde"/>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>&koboldcpp;</term>
<listitem><para>
A program that runs AI models on your own hardware: on this computer or on
another one in your network. Nothing leaves your network. See
<xref linkend="service-koboldcpp"/>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>OpenAI-compatible services</term>
<listitem><para>
Most cloud services (OpenAI, OpenRouter, Mistral, Groq and others) and many
local servers (Ollama, llama.cpp, LocalAI) speak the same interface. You
need the address of the service and usually an API key. See
<xref linkend="service-openai"/>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The &kappname; main window</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="main-window.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The &kappname; main window</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<important><para>
Everything you type and every file you attach is sent to the service you
have selected. With the &horde; that is the computer of a volunteer you do
not know; with a cloud service it is the company running it. Do not send
passwords, private documents or anything else you would not hand to a
stranger, unless the service is a &koboldcpp; or other server that you run
yourself.
</para></important>

<para>
What a service can do differs. &kappname; asks for or knows the abilities of
each service and only offers what the selected one supports:
</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="4">
<thead>
<row><entry></entry><entry>&horde;</entry><entry>&koboldcpp;</entry><entry>OpenAI-compatible</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>Chat</entry><entry>yes</entry><entry>yes</entry><entry>yes</entry></row>
<row><entry>Text files as attachments</entry><entry>yes</entry><entry>yes</entry><entry>yes</entry></row>
<row><entry>Images as attachments</entry><entry>yes, described in words first</entry><entry>if a vision model is loaded</entry><entry>yes, if the model can see</entry></row>
<row><entry>Audio as attachments</entry><entry>no</entry><entry>if a speech recognition or audio model is loaded</entry><entry>yes, directly or transcribed first</entry></row>
<row><entry>Video as attachments</entry><entry>no</entry><entry>no</entry><entry>yes, if the model can watch</entry></row>
<row><entry>Other files (PDF, ...)</entry><entry>no</entry><entry>no</entry><entry>yes, if the service accepts them</entry></row>
<row><entry>Generating images</entry><entry>yes</entry><entry>if an image model is loaded</entry><entry>yes, if the service offers it</entry></row>
<row><entry>Image from an image</entry><entry>yes</entry><entry>if an image model is loaded</entry><entry>no</entry></row>
<row><entry>Text to speech</entry><entry>no</entry><entry>if a speech model is loaded</entry><entry>yes, if the service offers it</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="using">
<title>Using &kappname;</title>

<sect1 id="main-window">
<title>The Main Window</title>

<para>The main window is made of these parts:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>The tool bar</term>
<listitem><para>
From left to right: <guiicon>New Chat</guiicon>, <guiicon>Attach
Files</guiicon>, the <guilabel>Service</guilabel> box where you choose who
answers, the <guilabel>Task</guilabel> box (<guilabel>Chat</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Generate Image</guilabel> or <guilabel>Text to Speech</guilabel>;
only what the service can do is listed), the <guilabel>Model</guilabel> box
and <guiicon>Reload Models</guiicon>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The list of chats</term>
<listitem><para>
On the left, with the chat you used last at the top. Click a chat to read or
continue it. Right-click it to rename, export or delete it.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The conversation</term>
<listitem><para>
Your messages and the answers. Pictures are shown in the conversation; click
one to open it in your image viewer. Other files appear as links that open in
the program &tde; uses for that kind of file. Files that came from the service
have a <guilabel>Save As...</guilabel> link next to them.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The message box</term>
<listitem><para>
At the bottom. Type here and press &Enter; or click
<guibutton>Send</guibutton>. Press <keycombo
action="simul">&Shift;&Enter;</keycombo> to start a new line. The
<guibutton>Stop</guibutton> button ends an answer that is on its way, and
<guibutton>Attach...</guibutton> adds files to the message.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The status bar</term>
<listitem><para>
Shows what is happening on the left (for example your position in the queue
of the &horde;) and the service, its address and your account on the right.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="chatting">
<title>Chatting</title>

<procedure>
<step><para>
Choose a service in the <guilabel>Service</guilabel> box. The first time you
use &koboldcpp; or an OpenAI-compatible service you have to tell &kappname;
where it is; see <xref linkend="configuration"/>.
</para></step>
<step><para>
Optionally choose a model in the <guilabel>Model</guilabel> box. With
<guilabel>(Default model)</guilabel> the service decides. You can also type
the name of a model that is not in the list and press &Enter;.
</para></step>
<step><para>
Type your message and press &Enter;.
</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>
The answer appears in the conversation. Services that can do so send it word
by word as it is written; the &horde; delivers it in one piece when a worker
has finished. The name of the model that answered is shown next to
<guilabel>AI</guilabel>.
</para>

<para>
The service receives the whole conversation with every message, so the AI
knows what was said before. To start afresh with nothing remembered, choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>New Chat</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.
</para>

<para>
If you are not happy with an answer, choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Message</guimenu><guimenuitem>Answer Again</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo>). The last
answer is thrown away and your message is sent again; you may change the
service, the task or the model first.
</para>

<para>
Answers often use a simple notation for emphasis and program text.
&kappname; shows <literal>**bold**</literal> as bold, <literal>*italic*</literal>
as italic, lists with bullets and program text in a box with a fixed-width
font.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="attachments">
<title>Attaching Files</title>

<para>There are four ways to attach files to the message you are writing:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>click <guibutton>Attach...</guibutton> or press <keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo> and select one or more
files;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>drag files from <application>Konqueror</application> or the
desktop and drop them on the conversation or on the message
box;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>copy files in <application>Konqueror</application>, or copy a
picture in an image program, and paste into the message box with <keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>V</keycap></keycombo>;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>name the files on the command line; see <xref
linkend="command-line"/>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
Attached files are listed above the message box. Click one to remove it
again. They are sent together with your next message.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A message with attachments</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="attachments.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>A message with attachments</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>What happens to a file depends on its kind:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>Text</term>
<listitem><para>
Text files, program sources, scripts, configuration files and the like are
put into the message under their name. This works with every service. Only
the first 256&nbsp;KB of a very large file are sent.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Images</term>
<listitem><para>
Sent as pictures to services whose model can see. Text models on the &horde;
cannot see: there the picture is first given to one of the Horde's image
description workers, and the description is passed on to the text model. The
AI then knows roughly what is in the picture, but no details.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Audio</term>
<listitem><para>
Sent as sound to models that can listen. &koboldcpp; servers without such a
model but with speech recognition, and OpenAI-compatible services if you
turned on <guilabel>Transcribe audio attachments before sending
them</guilabel>, turn the recording into text first; the AI then gets the
text.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Video and other files</term>
<listitem><para>
Sent as they are to OpenAI-compatible services. Whether the service can do
something with them depends on the service and the model; if not, it answers
with an error message.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
A file the selected service cannot take is marked with a warning sign in the
list. When you send the message &kappname; tells you which files will be left
out and lets you cancel.
</para>

<note><para>
Files are sent in full with every later message of the same chat, because
the services do not remember anything between messages. Large pictures and
recordings therefore make every message of that chat slower and, with
services that charge for use, more expensive.
</para></note>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="images">
<title>Generating Images</title>

<procedure>
<step><para>
Select <guilabel>Generate Image</guilabel> in the <guilabel>Task</guilabel>
box. If it is not in the list, the selected service cannot make images.
</para></step>
<step><para>
Optionally choose an image model in the <guilabel>Model</guilabel> box.
</para></step>
<step><para>
Describe the picture you want and click <guibutton>Generate</guibutton>.
</para></step>
</procedure>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A generated image</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="image-generation.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>A generated image</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The picture appears in the conversation. Click it to open it at full size;
use <guilabel>Save As...</guilabel> to keep a copy where you want it. The
size of the picture and other details are set on the
<guilabel>Generation</guilabel> page of the configuration dialog.
</para>

<para>
With the &horde; and &koboldcpp; you can attach a picture as the starting
point: the result is then a changed version of it, as described by your
text. How far it may differ is set by <guilabel>Change of an attached
image</guilabel>.
</para>

<para>
Each request makes one picture and stands for itself: unlike in a chat, the
service does not see your earlier descriptions.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="speech">
<title>Text to Speech</title>

<para>
Select <guilabel>Text to Speech</guilabel> in the <guilabel>Task</guilabel>
box, type the text and click <guibutton>Speak</guibutton>. The service sends
back a sound file, which appears as a link in the conversation. Click it to
play it in your audio player, or use <guilabel>Save As...</guilabel>.
</para>
<para>
The voice is chosen in the configuration dialog, on the page of the service.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="chats">
<title>Managing Chats</title>

<para>
Chats are saved automatically after every answer. The following can be done
with the chat that is shown, from the <guimenu>File</guimenu> menu or by
right-clicking it in the list:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Export Chat...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>
Writes the chat to a web page (<filename>.html</filename>) or a plain text
file (<filename>.txt</filename>), depending on the file name you give. The
web page shows pictures from where they are stored on this computer; they
are not copied.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Rename Chat...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>
Chats are named after their first message. This gives the chat another name.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Delete Chat</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>
Removes the chat for good, after asking. Pictures and sounds that the
service made for it stay in the data folder; see <xref linkend="files"/>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="services">
<title>The Services</title>

<sect1 id="service-horde">
<title>&horde;</title>

<para>
The &horde; is a community project. People with capable graphics cards run
<quote>workers</quote> that take requests from a common queue. Using it costs
nothing, and &kappname; works with it straight away, without an account.
</para>

<para>
Because the work is shared, you wait in a queue. The status bar and the
conversation show your position and an estimate of the time left. Short
waits are usual for text; images and busy times can take a few minutes.
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>Kudos and API keys</term>
<listitem><para>
The Horde keeps count of what people give and take in <quote>kudos</quote>.
Requests from people with more kudos are served first. Without an account
you use the anonymous key <literal>0000000000</literal> and are served last.
You can register for free at <ulink
url="https://aihorde.net/register">aihorde.net/register</ulink>, enter the
key you get on the <guilabel>AI Horde</guilabel> page of the configuration
dialog, and earn kudos, for example by running a worker yourself. Your user
name and kudos are shown in the status bar.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Models</term>
<listitem><para>
The <guilabel>Model</guilabel> box lists the models that workers offer right
now, those with the most workers first. With <guilabel>(Default
model)</guilabel> any worker may answer, which is fastest.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Prompt format</term>
<listitem><para>
Text models on the Horde receive the conversation as one piece of text in
which the turns are marked. Models are trained with different markings; if
answers look odd, contain <literal>###</literal> lines or the AI talks to
itself, try another <guilabel>Prompt format</guilabel>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Limits</term>
<listitem><para>
Anonymous requests are limited by the Horde: answers of at most 512 tokens
and images of moderate size and number of steps. If a request is larger than
any available worker accepts, &kappname; tells you so.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="service-koboldcpp">
<title>&koboldcpp;</title>

<para>
<ulink url="https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp">&koboldcpp;</ulink> is a
single program that runs language models in the GGUF format, and optionally
image, speech recognition and speech models. It is not part of
&kappname;: install and start it yourself, on this computer or on a more
powerful one in your network.
</para>

<procedure>
<step><para>
Start &koboldcpp; with a model. To reach it from other computers it must
listen on the network, for example
<userinput><command>koboldcpp</command> --model model.gguf --host 0.0.0.0 --port 5001</userinput>.
</para></step>
<step><para>
In &kappname; open
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure TDE AI Client...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>,
go to the <guilabel>KoboldCpp</guilabel> page and enter the address, for
example <literal>http://localhost:5001</literal> or
<literal>http://192.168.1.20:5001</literal>.
</para></step>
<step><para>
Select <guilabel>KoboldCpp</guilabel> in the <guilabel>Service</guilabel>
box.
</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>
&kappname; asks the server what it can do. The tasks and kinds of attachment
it offers depend on what &koboldcpp; was started with: a vision projector
(<option>--mmproj</option>) for pictures, a Whisper model
(<option>--whispermodel</option>) for recordings, an image model
(<option>--sdmodel</option>) for generating images and a speech model
(<option>--ttsmodel</option>) for text to speech. After restarting
&koboldcpp; with other options, click <guiicon>Reload Models</guiicon>.
</para>

<para>
The status bar shows the version of &koboldcpp; when the connection works.
If it cannot be reached, the status bar says why.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="service-openai">
<title>OpenAI-compatible Services</title>

<para>
On the <guilabel>OpenAI API</guilabel> page of the configuration dialog enter
the base address of the service's interface and your API key. The address
normally ends in <literal>/v1</literal>; some common ones can be picked from
the list:
</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Service</entry><entry>Address</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>OpenAI</entry><entry><literal>https://api.openai.com/v1</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>OpenRouter</entry><entry><literal>https://openrouter.ai/api/v1</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>Mistral</entry><entry><literal>https://api.mistral.ai/v1</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>Groq</entry><entry><literal>https://api.groq.com/openai/v1</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>Ollama on this computer</entry><entry><literal>http://localhost:11434/v1</literal></entry></row>
<row><entry>llama.cpp server on this computer</entry><entry><literal>http://localhost:8080/v1</literal></entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<para>
Local servers usually need no key; leave the field empty. Cloud services
normally charge for use; consult the service about its prices.
</para>

<para>
Click <guiicon>Reload Models</guiicon> to fetch the list of models and
choose one in the <guilabel>Model</guilabel> box. Most services require a
model to be chosen; if you leave <guilabel>(Default model)</guilabel> for a
chat, the first model of the list is used. The list contains all models of the service
for every task, because the service does not tell which model can do what:
for <guilabel>Generate Image</guilabel> pick an image model, for
<guilabel>Text to Speech</guilabel> a speech model.
</para>

<para>
Such a service cannot be asked what it is able to do, so &kappname; offers
every task and every kind of attachment. If the service or the chosen model
cannot handle something, the service's error message is shown in the
conversation.
</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="configuration">
<title>Configuration</title>

<para>
Choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure TDE AI Client...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
to open the configuration dialog. It has five pages.
</para>

<sect1 id="config-general">
<title>General</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Enter sends the message</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
When on, &Enter; sends and <keycombo action="simul">&Shift;&Enter;</keycombo>
starts a new line. When off, &Enter; starts a new line and <keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;&Enter;</keycombo> sends.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Keep API keys in the TDE wallet</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
API keys are normally stored in the configuration file of &kappname;, which
only you can read. With this option they are kept encrypted in
<application>TDEWallet</application> instead, in the folder
<literal>tde-ai-client</literal> of the network wallet. You may then be
asked for the wallet password when &kappname; starts. If the wallet
cannot be opened, &kappname; says so, turns the option off again and leaves
the keys in the configuration file.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Show API keys while this dialog is open</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Keys are hidden like passwords. Turn this on to check what you typed.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>System prompt</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Instructions that are given to the AI at the start of every conversation,
such as <quote>Answer briefly and in German.</quote> Leave it empty to send
none.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="config-horde">
<title>AI Horde</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The AI Horde settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings-horde.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The AI Horde settings</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Address</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Where the Horde is reached. Change it only to use another installation of
the Horde software. The default is
<literal>https://aihorde.net/api/v2</literal>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>API key</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Your key, or <literal>0000000000</literal> (ten zeros) to stay anonymous.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Prompt format</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
How the turns of the conversation are marked for text models:
<guilabel>Alpaca</guilabel> (suits most models), <guilabel>ChatML</guilabel>
or <guilabel>Plain</guilabel>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Use trusted workers only</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Only workers that have been part of the Horde for a while may do your
requests. Safer, but the wait is longer.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Also use slow workers</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
When off, slow workers are skipped. This costs extra kudos.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Allow images that are not safe for work</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
When off, workers are asked to replace such images.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="config-koboldcpp">
<title>KoboldCpp</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The KoboldCpp settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings-koboldcpp.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The KoboldCpp settings</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Address</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Address and port of the &koboldcpp; server, for example
<literal>http://localhost:5001</literal>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Password</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Only needed if &koboldcpp; was started with <option>--password</option>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Speech voice</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The voice for text to speech. &koboldcpp; knows a few by name, such as
<literal>kobo</literal>, <literal>cheery</literal>,
<literal>sleepy</literal> and <literal>shouty</literal>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="config-openai">
<title>OpenAI API</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The settings for OpenAI-compatible services</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings-openai.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The settings for OpenAI-compatible services</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Address</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The base address of the interface; see <xref linkend="service-openai"/>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>API key</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The key the service gave you. Empty for servers that need none.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Speech model</guilabel> and <guilabel>Speech voice</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Used for <guilabel>Text to Speech</guilabel> when
<guilabel>(Default model)</guilabel> is selected, for example
<literal>tts-1</literal> and <literal>alloy</literal>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Transcribe audio attachments before sending them</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Audio files are normally passed to the chat model, which only works with
models that can listen. With this option they are first turned into text by
the <guilabel>Transcription model</guilabel> (for example
<literal>whisper-1</literal>), and the text is sent.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="config-generation">
<title>Generation</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The generation settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings-generation.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The generation settings</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>These settings apply to all services.</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Longest answer</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The most the AI may write in one answer, in tokens (a token is about three
quarters of a word). Answers that reach it are cut off.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Context sent to AI Horde models</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
How much of the conversation the Horde passes to the model. Workers that
cannot handle this much do not take the request, so large values mean fewer
workers and longer waits.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Temperature</guilabel> and <guilabel>Top-P</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
How freely the AI chooses its words. Low values give predictable, repeatable
answers; higher values more varied and surprising ones.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Width</guilabel> and <guilabel>Height</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The size of generated images. The &horde; and &koboldcpp; work with multiples
of 64; other values are rounded. Cloud services often accept only a few
fixed sizes, such as 1024&nbsp;x&nbsp;1024.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Steps</guilabel> and <guilabel>Guidance</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
More steps give finer pictures and take longer. Guidance says how closely the
picture follows your description. Used by the &horde; and &koboldcpp;.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Change of an attached image</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
When you attach a picture as the starting point: 0 keeps it as it is, 1
ignores it.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Negative prompt</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
What should not appear in generated images, for example <quote>blurry, text,
watermark</quote>. Used by the &horde; and &koboldcpp;.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command Reference</title>

<sect1 id="menus">
<title>Menus and Shortcuts</title>

<variablelist>
<title>The <guimenu>File</guimenu> Menu</title>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>N</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>New Chat</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Starts a new, empty chat.</action></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Export Chat...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Writes the chat to a web page or text file.</action></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F2</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Rename Chat...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Gives the chat another name.</action></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>D</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Delete Chat</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Deletes the chat</action> after asking.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Q</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Quit</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Closes &kappname;.</action></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<variablelist>
<title>The <guimenu>Edit</guimenu> Menu</title>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>C</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Copy</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Copies the text selected</action> in the conversation or the message box.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<variablelist>
<title>The <guimenu>Message</guimenu> Menu</title>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Enter;</keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Message</guimenu><guimenuitem>Send</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Sends the message</action> with its attachments.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut>&Esc;</shortcut>
<guimenu>Message</guimenu><guimenuitem>Stop</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Stops the answer</action> that is on its way. What has arrived so far is kept.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Message</guimenu><guimenuitem>Answer Again</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Discards the last answer and asks again.</action></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Message</guimenu><guimenuitem>Attach Files...</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Adds files</action> to the message you are writing.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Message</guimenu><guimenuitem>Remove All Attachments</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Empties the list of attached files.</action></para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<variablelist>
<title>The <guimenu>Service</guimenu> Menu</title>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>AI Horde</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>KoboldCpp</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>OpenAI-compatible</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Select the service</action> that answers. The same as the <guilabel>Service</guilabel> box in the tool bar.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>Chat</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>Generate Image</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>Text to Speech</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Select the task.</action> Tasks the service cannot do are greyed out.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F5</keycap></shortcut>
<guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>Reload Models</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><action>Asks the service again</action> for its models and abilities.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
The <guimenu>Settings</guimenu> and <guimenu>Help</guimenu> menus contain
the entries common to &tde; programs, among them
<guimenuitem>Configure Shortcuts...</guimenuitem> to change the keys listed
above.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line">
<title>Command Line Options</title>

<cmdsynopsis>
<command>tde-ai-client</command>
<arg>-b, --backend <replaceable>service</replaceable></arg>
<arg>-m, --mode <replaceable>mode</replaceable></arg>
<arg>-p, --prompt <replaceable>text</replaceable></arg>
<arg rep="repeat"><replaceable>file</replaceable></arg>
</cmdsynopsis>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>-b</option>, <option>--backend</option> <replaceable>service</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>
Select the service: <literal>horde</literal>, <literal>koboldcpp</literal>
or <literal>openai</literal>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>-m</option>, <option>--mode</option> <replaceable>mode</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>
Select the task: <literal>chat</literal>, <literal>image</literal> or
<literal>speech</literal>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>-p</option>, <option>--prompt</option> <replaceable>text</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>
Send <replaceable>text</replaceable> as soon as the program has started.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><replaceable>file</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>
Files to attach to the first message.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>For example, to ask your &koboldcpp; server about a picture:</para>
<screen><prompt>%</prompt> <userinput><command>tde-ai-client</command> --backend koboldcpp --prompt "What is this?" photo.jpg</userinput></screen>

<para>
The generic &tde; and &TQt; options are accepted as well; see
<userinput><command>tde-ai-client</command> --help</userinput>.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="files">
<title>Files</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename>$TDEHOME/share/config/tde-ai-clientrc</filename></term>
<listitem><para>
The settings, including the API keys unless they are kept in the wallet. The
file is readable by you only.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename class="directory">$TDEHOME/share/apps/tde-ai-client/conversations/</filename></term>
<listitem><para>
The chats, one file each.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename class="directory">$TDEHOME/share/apps/tde-ai-client/media/</filename></term>
<listitem><para>
Pictures and sounds received from services, and pictures pasted from the
clipboard. Files are not removed when a chat is deleted; delete them here if
you no longer want them.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
<envar>TDEHOME</envar> is normally <filename
class="directory">~/.trinity</filename>.
</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="faq">
<title>Questions and Answers</title>

<qandaset>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The &horde; takes very long to answer.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Anonymous requests come last in the queue. Select <guilabel>(Default
model)</guilabel> so that any worker can answer, lower <guilabel>Longest
answer</guilabel>, and consider registering for an API key; see <xref
linkend="service-horde"/>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The &horde; says no worker can do my request.</para></question>
<answer><para>
No worker that is online offers the chosen model with the requested answer
length, context or image size. Choose another model or
<guilabel>(Default model)</guilabel>, or lower those values on the
<guilabel>Generation</guilabel> page.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Answers from the &horde; contain <literal>###</literal> lines or
the AI continues the conversation by itself.</para></question>
<answer><para>
The model expects another prompt format. Change <guilabel>Prompt
format</guilabel> on the <guilabel>AI Horde</guilabel> page.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>&koboldcpp; cannot be reached.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Check that it is running and that the address and port are right. A server on
another computer must have been started with <option>--host
0.0.0.0</option> (or that computer's address), and a firewall on it must let
the port through. Opening the address in a web browser should show the
&koboldcpp; page.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para><guilabel>Generate Image</guilabel> or <guilabel>Text to
Speech</guilabel> is missing from the <guilabel>Task</guilabel> box.</para></question>
<answer><para>
The selected service does not offer it: the &horde; has no text to speech,
and &koboldcpp; only offers what it was started with models for. See <xref
linkend="service-koboldcpp"/>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>I get <quote>HTTP error 401</quote> or a message about the API key.</para></question>
<answer><para>
The service rejected your key. Enter it again on the page of the service;
turn on <guilabel>Show API keys while this dialog is open</guilabel> to see
what you type.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The service answers with an error when I attach a picture, a
recording or a video.</para></question>
<answer><para>
The chosen model cannot work with that kind of file. Choose a model that
can; for recordings with an OpenAI-compatible service you can also turn on
<guilabel>Transcribe audio attachments before sending them</guilabel>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Can &kappname; make videos?</para></question>
<answer><para>
No. Videos can be attached for services whose models understand them, but
none of the supported interfaces is used to create them.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Does &kappname; work through a proxy?</para></question>
<answer><para>
Yes. It uses the network settings of &tde;: set the proxy in the Control
Centre under <guilabel>Internet &amp; Network</guilabel>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

